          "prompt": "Stylized hand-painted 3D game environment art for a fast first-person roguelite shooter, matching the\nreadable visual direction of Gunfire Reborn: bold chunky silhouettes, broad clean faceted planes, two or three dominant\ncolour blocks, soft painted gradients and a few restrained edge accents. East-Asian Silk Road frontier fantasy of the Han-dynasty Gobi desert: ochre-red sandstone and wind-carved yardang rock with horizontal strata, pale sand-yellow rammed earth with reed layers, weathered grey-brown timber, and only a few faded Dunhuang mural accents in malachite green, lapis blue and vermilion. Matte stone,\nwood and cloth, restrained highlights on metal, sparse painted wear. No photorealism, micro-grain, dense tiny cracks,\nelaborate miniature carvings, harsh specular highlights or baked cast shadows. Even neutral lighting.\n\nAsset (8m wide x 7.5m tall x 5m deep): One straight 8m bay segment of a two-storey Tang-dynasty temple cloister gallery, 8m long, 5m deep and 7.5m tall, designed to repeat in a row. The BACK is a solid thick pale sand-yellow plastered mud-brick wall running the full length and height. The FRONT is open: GROUND FLOOR a row of three thick round timber columns (faded vermilion lacquer, chunky stone bases) standing 2.6m apart, carrying an upper floor deck at 3.5m whose thick timber edge beam shows across the front; UPPER FLOOR a covered walkway along the front with three more columns and a chunky low timber railing 0.9m tall; ROOF a single-slope grey-brown tiled lean-to roof sloping from the back wall down toward the front, with deep eaves and chunky rafter ends, reaching 7.5m at the back. Both ends of the segment are flat vertical cuts through the floor, roof and back wall so identical segments join seamlessly in a line (no end columns or end walls). Faded vermilion columns, weathered grey-brown timber, grey-brown tiles, pale plaster, a faded malachite-green and lapis-blue band under the eaves. PROPORTIONS: FRONT and BACK views drawn about 1.05 times as wide as tall; side views show the lean-to section, the open front and the solid back wall, about 0.7 times as wide as tall. Broad faceted hand-painted planes, chunky forms, no lanterns, banners or debris. One solid connected piece on a flat base.\n\nOutput: one orthographic model turnaround sheet for 3D reconstruction. Exactly four separate complete views\nof the SAME object in one row, left to right:\n1. FRONT view: the object's front faces the viewer.\n2. LEFT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the LEFT edge of the image (camera at the\n   object's right-hand side).\n3. BACK view: the object's back faces the viewer.\n4. RIGHT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the RIGHT edge of the image.\nSame scale and same ground line in all four, each centred in its quarter with generous empty margin, never touching or\noverlapping; the whole object fits inside its quarter, nothing cropped.\nPlain flat light neutral grey background (#D6D6D6). No floor, no ground shadow, no text, no labels, no frames, no\nperspective.",
